#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/id #uuid "76b310bf-b592-541c-82e8-93d72bc688f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21778" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x)$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p'$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'$.\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= (u_{1}\\sin (4x) + u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= (u_{1}\\sin (4x))^{′} + (u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p'$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147167 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "8587baf6-8f11-56eb-8afa-dd385c0aeaeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21779" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x)$,\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n$y_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2'$, and $p(x){:}$\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p'$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} + 5y^{′} - 6y &= p(x) \\\\\n(y_{p}^{′})^{′} + 5y_{p}^{′} - 6y_{p} &= p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p'$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{-6x})^{′})^{′} + 5(u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{-6x})^{′}) - 6(u_{1}e^{x} + u_{2}e^{-6x}) &= p(x) \\\\\n(u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x})^{′} + 5u_{1}e^{x} - 30u_{2}e^{-6x} - 6u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x} &= p(x) \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}e^{x} + u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}^{′}e^{-6x} + 36u_{2}e^{-6x} \\\\\n+ 5u_{1}e^{x} - 30u_{2}e^{-6x} - 6u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x} &= p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\nu_{1}(e^{x} + 5e^{x} - 6e^{x})_(⏟)_(0) + u_{2}(36e^{-6x} - 30e^{-6x} - 6e^{-6x})_(⏟)_(0) + u_{1}^{′}e^{x} - 6u_{2}^{′}e^{-6x} = p(x)\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "cb472181-da8e-5252-9c80-4d336e2ccf9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6615" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 9y = 6\\cos x$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x)$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution\n$y_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2$\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n${u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x$, find the function $g$." :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 6\\cos x\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos (3x))(\\sin (3x))^{′} - (\\cos (3x))^{′}(\\sin (3x)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (3x))(3\\cos (3x)) - (-3\\sin (3x))(\\sin (3x)) \\\\\n&= 3\\cos^{2} (3x) + 3\\sin^{2} (3x) \\\\\n&= 3 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}(\\cos (3x))^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(\\sin (3x))^{′} = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}\\cos (3x) + u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x) = 0⟹{-3u_{1}^{′}\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}\\cos (3x) + u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2$. We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3u_{1}^{′}\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n- 3(- (u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x))/(\\cos (3x)))\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(\\sin^{2} (3x))/(\\cos (3x)) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(\\sin^{2} (3x) + \\cos^{2} (3x))/(\\cos (3x)) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(1)/(\\cos (3x)) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\nu_{2}^{′} &= 2\\cos x\\cos (3x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫u_{2}^{′}dx &= ∫2\\cos x\\cos (3x)dx \\\\\nu_{2} &= ∫2\\cos x\\cos (3x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x)\n$$"}]}
